{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Sally goes on a Safari"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "African Animals"}, {"type": "position", "content": 2}, {"type": "origin", "content": "scholar > Nature Around Us > Safari"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 8}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.2"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Accurately use information about people's jobs and places of work in communicative situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "On a Safari"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "A safari is a trip to observe wild animals, especially in Africa. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you talk about a safari, you use the verb \u201cto go\u201d: </br> - <span class=\"italic\">I went on a safari.</span>  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You talk about the different animals, for example: </br>  - <span class=\"italic\"> I saw many elephants or I didn't see any lions. </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can talk about the geographic features, for example:  </br> - <span class=\"italic\">I saw many hills. </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you talk to someone about a safari you went on, they can ask questions like:  </br> - <span class=\"italic\">Which country did you go to?</span> </br>- <span class=\"italic\"> What did you see? </span> </br>- <span class=\"italic\">Did you see any lions? </span>"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Comparatives and Superlatives"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use adjectives to compare two things. These are called  <span class=\"strong\">comparative</span> adjectives. You can also use adjectives to compare one item with the rest of a group. These are called <span class=\"strong\">superlative</span> adjectives."}, {"type": "example", "content": "The lion is smaller than the tiger."}, {"type": "text", "content": " For regular comparative adjectives, you can use the structure:  <br/> <ul><li> adjective (<span class=\"strong\">-er</span>) <span class=\"strong\">than</span><br/>  Use this when the adjective in question is a short, one-syllable word.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">small &#8594; smaller</span></li> <li>adjective (<span class=\"strong\">-ier</span>) <span class=\"strong\">than</span><br/>  Use this if the adjective ends in <span class=\"italic\">-y</span> and has two syllables.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">happy &#8594; happier</span></li> <li><span class=\"strong\">more/less</span> + adjective + <span class=\"strong\">than</span><br/> Use this if the adjective has 2 or more syllables. The adjective does not change. <br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">careful &#8594; more careful</span>, <span class=\"italic\">boring &#8594; less boring</span><br/> There are a few exceptions, however: <span class=\"italic\">simple &#8594; simpler</span>, <span class=\"italic\">narrow  &#8594; narrower</span></li> <li><span class=\"strong\">as</span> + adjective + <span class=\"strong\">as</span> <br/> Use this when comparing two things that are the same regarding the adjective in question. The adjective does not change.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">I'm as fast as you. </span></li></ul>"}, {"type": "text", "content": " For regular superlative adjectives, you can use the structure:<br/>  <ul><li> <span class=\"strong\">the</span> adjective (<span class=\"strong\">-est</span>)<br/> Use this when the adjective in question is a short, one-syllable word.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">small &#8594; smallest</span></li> <li><span class=\"strong\">the</span> adjective (<span class=\"strong\">-iest</span>)<br/> Use this if the word ends in <span class=\"italic\">-y</span> and has two syllables.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">happy &#8594; happiest</span> <span class=\"strong\">the most/least</span> + adjective<br/></li> <li><span class=\"strong\">the most/least</span> adjective <br/>Use this if the adjective has 2 or more syllables. The adjective does not change.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">careful &#8594; most careful</span>, <span class=\"italic\">boring &#8594; most boring</span><br/> There are a few exceptions, however: <span class=\"italic\">simple &#8594; simplest</span>, <span class=\"italic\">narrow &#8594; narrowest</span></li></ul> </li></ul>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "The cheetah is the fastest animal in the cat family."}, {"type": "example", "content": "It was the best trip!"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "leopard"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a member of the cat family, with yellow fur and black spots."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Leopards are slower than lions."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn5le08ob02aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear on a safari."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to hunt "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to chase animals and kill them"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The lion hunts at night."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn5le08ob02aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "cheetah"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a member of the cat family, with long legs and black spots."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The cheetah is the fastest animal in the world."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn5le08ob02aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to rest"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to lie down to relax"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The lions rest under the shade."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn5le08ob02aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "mane"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the long hair around the face and neck of a lion"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The lion's mane blows in the wind."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn5le08ob02aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tiger"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a large member of the cat family, with orange and black lines on its body "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:  The tiger is larger than the lion."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn5le08ob02aud06"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "trunk"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the long nose of an elephant"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The elephant lifted its trunk."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn5le08ob02aud07"}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn5le08ob02re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Go on the internet and find out about all the members of the cat family."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Choose one animal."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write 5-6 sentences about the animal you chose. Describe it."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Now choose another type of cat. Compare this animal to one you wrote about."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write 3 sentences about how they are the same."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write 2 sentences about how they are different. Remember to use comparatives and superlatives."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read your sentences aloud to the class."}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I research two members of the cat family."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I compare two animals."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I read my sentences aloud."}]}], "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"_data": [], "template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"id": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "_data": [], "template_override": 1, "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "engenn5le08ob02re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn5le08ob02re2img05.jpg", "images": [{"text": "", "id": 0, "time": "0", "image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img05.jpg"}, {"text": "<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Hey, Sally! What did you do today? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: I went on an African safari! It was the best! </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Cool. What did you see?", "id": 1, "time": "100", "image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img05.jpg"}, {"text": "<span class=\"strong\">Sally:</span> We saw lions. They hunt together at night, not like leopards. Leopards  hunt alone.  Lions are faster than leopards, too. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Not as fast as cheetahs, though. They're the fastest! </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: The lions rest in the shade during the day. They looked magnificent lying there with their manes blowing.", "id": 2, "time": "10500", "image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img06.jpg"}, {"text": "<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Only male lions have manes, right? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: That's right. Did you know that lions are smaller than tigers? I always thought lions were bigger. Turns out the tigers are the largest. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: I didn't. Did you see  any other animals?", "id": 3, "time": "31000", "image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img07.jpg"}, {"text": "<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: Yes. We went on elephant rides.  It was the most amazing experience. We fed them bananas, and the coolest part was to see the elephants lift their trunks. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Sounds like you had a blast!", "id": 4, "time": "48000", "image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img08.jpg"}]}}]}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "id": 0, "type": "WLC", "img_path": "", "_data"